config SERVO_TRACKER
    bool "Pan and tilt servo tracking"
    default n
    help
      Turns a pan and tilt mount to keep the tracked hand in the middle of the
      frame, driving hobby servos over the kernel's sysfs PWM interface.
      Without it the application only watches; nothing moves.

      Nothing reports where the mount is pointing, so the loop closes through
      the camera: the error it drives to zero is how far the target sits from
      the middle of the frame.

if SERVO_TRACKER

choice
    prompt "Servo kind"
    default SERVO_TRACKER_KIND_POSITIONAL
    help
      What the pulse width means to the servo, which is a property of the
      hardware rather than a preference.

config SERVO_TRACKER_KIND_POSITIONAL
    bool "Positional (MG90S, SG90, most servos)"
    help
      The pulse is an angle. The servo goes there and holds it against its
      internal potentiometer. The loop adds the frame's correction to the
      angle it already holds, because commanding the angle proportionally to
      the error would settle wherever the error balanced the gain and leave
      the target permanently off centre.

      Travel is finite, so set the span to the mount's real range: a servo
      asked past its end stop stalls there and keeps drawing its stall
      current, around 700 mA on an MG90S.

config SERVO_TRACKER_KIND_CONTINUOUS
    bool "Continuous rotation (360 degree, FS90R, modified servos)"
    help
      The pulse is a speed: the servo turns one way above neutral and the
      other way below, faster the further from neutral, and reports nothing.
      The mount's angle is the integral of that speed, so proportional control
      alone leaves no steady state error.

      These ignore pulses within a few tens of microseconds of neutral, so
      set the dead band and the loop will step over it.

endchoice

config SERVO_TRACKER_PWM_DEVICE
    string "PWM controller"
    default "11080000.pwm"
    help
      The PWM block whose outputs drive the servos, named as the kernel names
      the device. Its /sys/class/pwm/pwmchipN is found at startup by this
      name, because N depends on the order the drivers probe in. 11080000.pwm
      is PWM1, whose channel number is the N in PWM1_OUTN.

      On the Cove-610 every PWM1 output lands on an LCD pin of the JP2 header:

        channel  GPIO     JP2 pin  used by the LCD as
        0        GPIO7_7  22       backlight (PWM1_OUT0)
        1        GPIO1_0  21       TE, an output of the panel
        2        GPIO1_2  24       reset
        3        GPIO1_3  23       command/data select
        4        GPIO1_1  26       SPI data
        5        GPIO7_6  25       not used
        6        GPIO7_5  28       SPI clock
        7        GPIO7_4  27       SPI chip select

      The defaults put pan on channel 5 and tilt on channel 1, which leave the
      LCD driver's own pins alone. Channel 1 is the panel's TE output, so the
      panel has to be unplugged while the servos run.

config SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_ENABLE
    bool "Drive the pan axis"
    default y

config SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_CHANNEL
    int "Pan PWM channel"
    default 5
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_ENABLE
    help
      Channel 5 is GPIO7_6, JP2 pin 25, which the LCD does not use.

config SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_IOCFG
    hex "Pan pin IO config register"
    default 0x1113004C
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_ENABLE
    help
      Address of the register that muxes the pin, written once at startup
      through /dev/mem. 0x1113004C is GPIO7_6. Set this to 0 when the board's
      own device tree already muxes the pin.

config SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_IOCFG_VALUE
    hex "Pan pin IO config value"
    default 0x1007
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_ENABLE
    help
      Bits 3:0 select the function, 7:4 the drive strength, 8 and 9 the pulls,
      10 the slew rate, 12 the input enable. Function 7 is PWM1_OUT5 on this
      pin.

config SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_INVERT
    bool "Reverse the pan direction"
    default n
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_ENABLE
    help
      Set this if the mount turns away from the hand instead of towards it.
      Which way a servo turns for a given pulse depends on how it is geared
      and mounted, so this is quicker to try than rewiring.

config SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_ENABLE
    bool "Drive the tilt axis"
    default y

config SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_CHANNEL
    int "Tilt PWM channel"
    default 1
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_ENABLE
    help
      Channel 1 is GPIO1_0, JP2 pin 21, the LCD's TE line. Unplug the panel
      first: TE is driven by the panel, and the two outputs would fight.

config SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_IOCFG
    hex "Tilt pin IO config register"
    default 0x1113003C
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_ENABLE
    help
      0x1113003C is GPIO1_0. Set to 0 when the device tree already muxes it.

config SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_IOCFG_VALUE
    hex "Tilt pin IO config value"
    default 0x1007
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_ENABLE
    help
      Function 7 is PWM1_OUT1 on this pin.

config SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_INVERT
    bool "Reverse the tilt direction"
    default n
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_ENABLE

config SERVO_TRACKER_PERIOD_US
    int "PWM period in microseconds"
    default 20000
    range 1000 100000
    help
      20000 is the 50 Hz frame nearly every hobby servo expects. Digital
      servos often accept 5000 (200 Hz), which tightens the loop, but check
      the servo before changing this.

config SERVO_TRACKER_NEUTRAL_US
    int "Middle of the travel, in microseconds"
    default 1500
    range 500 3000
    help
      For a positional servo this is the angle the mount points straight on,
      and where it starts and is parked. For a continuous one it is the pulse
      at which the servo stops; if yours creeps while the frame is empty,
      trim this by a few microseconds.

config SERVO_TRACKER_SPAN_US
    int "Half the travel, in microseconds"
    default 500
    range 50 1000
    help
      The pulse is kept within this far either side of neutral, so 500 around
      a 1500 neutral means 1000 to 2000.

      On a positional servo this is a mechanical limit, not a preference: it
      has to be the range the MOUNT can reach, which is usually less than the
      servo can. Find the real ends with tools/servo_test before widening it,
      and start narrow. An MG90S reaches its full 180 degrees over roughly
      500 to 2500 microseconds, but the mount will stop it well before that,
      and the servo will stall against whatever stops it first.

      On a continuous servo it is the offset at full speed, and lowering it
      just caps how fast the mount can turn.

config SERVO_TRACKER_DEAD_BAND_US
    int "Servo dead band in microseconds"
    default 40
    range 0 300
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_KIND_CONTINUOUS
    help
      How far from neutral the pulse must be before the servo turns at all.
      Commanding less than this only makes it buzz, so the loop steps over
      the band rather than through it. Raise it if a stopped servo hums.

config SERVO_TRACKER_STEP_PERCENT
    int "Angle moved per frame at full command"
    default 15
    range 1 100
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_KIND_POSITIONAL
    help
      As a percentage of the span. This is the main control over how fast the
      mount follows, and it is worth doing the arithmetic rather than
      guessing, because three things divide into it.

      Travel a second = span * this% * gain * frame rate. The gain is there
      because the command only reaches 1 with the target right at the edge of
      the frame, and the frame rate is the inference rate, not the camera's:
      a frame with a hand in it costs around 55 ms here, so roughly 18 a
      second rather than 30.

      At 15%, a 500 us span and a gain of 100, that is 500 * 0.15 * 18 = 1350
      us a second with the target at the edge, crossing the whole 1000 us
      travel in about three quarters of a second. Lower it if the mount
      overshoots and hunts around the target.

config SERVO_TRACKER_RECENTRE_ON_LOSS
    bool "Return to the middle when the target is lost"
    default n
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_KIND_POSITIONAL
    help
      Off, the mount keeps pointing where it last saw the hand. On, it walks
      back to the middle of its travel at the tracking rate. A continuous
      servo cannot do this at all, having no position to return to.

config SERVO_TRACKER_GAIN_PERCENT
    int "Speed commanded with the target at the frame edge"
    default 60
    range 1 400
    help
      The proportional gain. Higher follows faster but overshoots, and the
      mount then hunts around the target because the camera sees each
      correction a frame late.

config SERVO_TRACKER_DEAD_ZONE_PERCENT
    int "Dead zone around the frame centre"
    default 8
    range 0 50
    help
      Half width of the region, as a percentage of half the frame, in which
      the servos stop. Without it the mount twitches at every jitter of the
      detection. Widen it if the mount never settles.

config SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_SPAN_US
    int "Pan travel override, in microseconds"
    default 0
    range 0 1000
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_ENABLE
    help
      Half the travel this axis is allowed, when the pan mount reaches less
      far than the shared span. 0 uses the shared span.

config SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_SPAN_US
    int "Tilt travel override, in microseconds"
    default 0
    range 0 1000
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_ENABLE
    help
      Half the travel this axis is allowed. Tilt is usually the axis that
      reaches less far than the servo does, so this is usually the one to
      set. 0 uses the shared span.

config SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_STEP_PERCENT
    int "Pan step override"
    default 0
    range 0 100
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_PAN_ENABLE && SERVO_TRACKER_KIND_POSITIONAL
    help
      This axis's own per frame step. 0 uses the shared step.

config SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_STEP_PERCENT
    int "Tilt step override"
    default 0
    range 0 100
    depends on SERVO_TRACKER_TILT_ENABLE && SERVO_TRACKER_KIND_POSITIONAL
    help
      This axis's own per frame step, for when tilt moves more sharply than
      pan at the same setting. It usually does: the error is measured against
      half the frame in each direction and the frame is wider than it is tall,
      so on a 640 by 480 frame the same movement of the hand reads a third
      larger vertically. 0 uses the shared step.

config SERVO_TRACKER_MAX_SPEED_PERCENT
    int "Command ceiling"
    default 70
    range 1 100

config SERVO_TRACKER_SLEW_PERCENT
    int "Largest speed change per frame"
    default 25
    range 1 100
    help
      Limits how abruptly the mount may change speed, which is what keeps the
      loop's one frame of delay from turning into overshoot.

endif
